  "response": "**Setting up the calculation (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(no D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|no D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(+|no D) = 0.05\n\n**P(test positive):**\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|no D)·P(no D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\n**P(D|+) via Bayes' theorem:**\nP(D|+) = (0.98 × 0.002) / 0.05186\nP(D|+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186\nP(D|+) ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Converting to a percentage:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly accurate test, because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
